 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query. The computation involves a constant number of arithmetic operations (absolute values, subtractions, max/min, and a square root) [G].
 **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No auxiliary data structures are required [G].
-**Measured Performance:** On modern GPUs, this is one of the fastest SDF primitives to evaluate. The square root operation is the most expensive part, but it is only computed when the point is outside the box [G].
-**Scaling:** The method scales linearly with the number of query points (e.g., in a ray marching loop). It is independent of scene complexity, as it only depends on the box parameters [G].
 **Strengths:** Exact Euclidean distance, fast evaluation, supports CSG operations (union, intersection, difference) via min/max operations, provides correct normals via gradient [G].
-**Limitations:** Only applies to axis-aligned boxes. For oriented boxes, a transformation matrix must be applied to the query point before evaluation, which adds computational cost [G]. The sharp corners can cause aliasing in ambient occlusion if the ray marching steps are too large [S13, unofficial].
-**When to use:** When rendering axis-aligned boxes, cubes, or rectangular prisms. Ideal for voxel-like structures, UI elements, or simple geometric primitives [G].
-**When not to use:** When the box is rotated (use a transformed point or a different primitive). When rounded corners are required (use the Box with Edge Rounding SDF) [G].
+**Limitations:** Only applies to axis-aligned boxes [S14, unofficial]. For oriented boxes, a transformation matrix must be applied to the query point before evaluation, which adds computational cost [G]. The sharp corners can cause aliasing in ambient occlusion if the ray marching steps are too large [S13, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Signed Box Distance Function is a fundamental primitive in the field of Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) and ray marching. It was popularized by Inigo Quilez in his online resources on distance functions [S2, unofficial]. The method exploits the symmetry of the box to reduce the computation to the first octant, a technique widely used in computer graphics for efficient geometric queries [S2, unofficial]. It is used in real-time rendering, implicit modeling, and collision detection [G]. Algorithm 3.5 underestimates the distance in corner regions, leading to smaller ray steps and incorrect ambient occlusion [S12, S13, unofficial]. Algorithm 3.6 fixes this by correctly calculating the outside distance, providing the true Euclidean distance required for accurate sphere tracing and ambient occlusion [S12, S13, unofficial].
